## Session Discipline

**Every new task — even the second or third in the same session — must read `routing.yaml` and re-match one route before acting.** Re-matching is cheap and catches the most common error: a new task that belongs to a different route than the last one.

- **Re-match the route every task** from canonical `routing.yaml`. Never assume the new task uses the last route.
- **Re-read only what changed or was lost** — after a route change, read the new workflow; after compaction, recover only the current workflow and decision-relevant evidence.
- **Use evidence-driven context** — start with the selected workflow and the smallest source slice that can decide the next action. Only one clear read-only or fixed-contract maintenance action/check with no new desired behavior stays Simple. Any request that adds or changes user-visible behavior, a business flow/state, or an external contract first reaches `requirement-ready`, projects Goal/Done When/Boundaries into a Task Anchor, proves the implementation owner and Current -> Target until `implementation-ready`, and only then uses a concise Native Plan before mutation. An incomplete Requirement returns current understanding, real risk/conflict, and the minimum normative question instead of a Plan.
- **Bind domains late** — explicit business-rule requests or source Plans that already declare the applicable business-domain owner may read `domain-routing.yaml` after workflow selection. Ambiguous work inspects evidence first; keywords identify candidates only. A source Plan is read directly by its workflow and does not by itself activate a domain. Clearly technical work never reads the domain manifest. A domain appends knowledge and cannot replace the workflow; load one owner by default and expand only on cross-domain evidence.
- **Load lifecycle contracts at their boundary** — modifying workflows read `rules/change-discipline.md` before the first mutation; adopted testing guidance loads when constructing evidence; `workflows/task-closure.md` loads only when completion begins.
- **If you cannot tell whether context was compacted, re-read** — partial memory is worse than no memory.
- Before final validation/commit after a long or interrupted task, run `protocol-blocks/reboot-check.md`: restate the original user constraints and chosen route; if you cannot, re-read the request, current workflow, and decision-relevant evidence before proceeding.

**Request-clarity judgment** — vague wording is a signal, not an automatic blocker. Use the smallest read-only evidence to derive scope and ask only for a remaining normative decision. See `protocol-blocks/ambiguous-request-gate.md`.

</always-applicable>

